;; Subject glossary — who can act in scenarios. Every scenario subject is
;; checked against this file at planning time.
;;
;; Instances are personas: give each a :desc docstring so humans and agents
;; know who they are. A persona may also wear a costume — a persistent
;; logged-in browser profile (:wears; see `sl costume init`).
;;
;; :adversary is a standing spur, not a mechanism: misuse cases are
;; authored AS this actor (`When :adversary …`). It ships as identity plus
;; :desc only — no capability bag, no personas — because the framework
;; does not know what YOUR adversaries can do; you declare that. Two
;; flavors, both subject types rather than new machinery; name them per
;; app (split the type, or add personas under it) when the distinction
;; matters:
;;
;;   - inside job — authenticated, holding a legitimate bag, hostile
;;     intent. Privilege escalation is probing the cells outside that bag;
;;     revocation (laid off, access not yet pulled) is the same subject run
;;     against their own former bag, expecting denials.
;;   - outside job — unauthenticated. The guest row plus authored misuse
;;     cases for attacks with novel steps.
;;
;; "AD authenticated them; it didn't authorize them." Delegating
;; authentication is the most common reason authorization goes untested;
;; the inside job is exactly the case that delegation cannot cover.
;;
;; Limit, stated plainly: scenarios authored as :adversary probe ROLE-level
;; access. They do not test object-level (horizontal) access — one user
;; reaching another user's records — which needs its own cases.
{:subjects
 {:user {:desc "Standard application user"
         :instances [{:id :alice
                      :desc "Baseline user — exercises the ordinary path"}
                     {:id :bob
                      :desc "Second actor — the counterparty in multi-user flows"}]}
  :adversary {:desc "Hostile actor — author misuse cases as this subject. Inside job (authenticated, legitimate bag, hostile intent) or outside job (unauthenticated). No capability bag is shipped: what an adversary can do is yours to declare. Covers role-level probing only, not object-level (horizontal) access."}}}
